WEBVTT

00:00:00.160 --> 00:00:02.920
Hello and welcome back to Paragliding Atlas Podcast.

00:00:02.920 --> 00:00:06.480
I'm your host, Aninder Singh, and joining us today is the man

00:00:06.480 --> 00:00:10.600
who needs no introduction. I'm talking about none other

00:00:10.600 --> 00:00:15.200
than Russell Ogden. Russ, how do I begin?

00:00:15.200 --> 00:00:18.640
This is beyond words. Such a huge honour and something

00:00:18.640 --> 00:00:20.960
that I'd looked forward to ever since I started flying.

00:00:21.080 --> 00:00:24.840
Thank you so much for coming. Well, thank you so long to be

00:00:24.840 --> 00:00:28.920
here and looking forward to it, Russ.

00:00:29.200 --> 00:00:32.640
I've been in the sport for like almost 7-8 years now and you

00:00:32.640 --> 00:00:37.080
know, one thing that I've always kind of sought after is

00:00:37.080 --> 00:00:40.840
information. And as I do, I of course, you

00:00:40.840 --> 00:00:44.400
know, look it up on the public domain, on the Internet and

00:00:44.400 --> 00:00:46.040
whatever search engines provide us.

00:00:47.440 --> 00:00:50.920
I'm very curious, are you the most interviewed person in

00:00:50.920 --> 00:00:54.080
paragliding? I have no idea.

00:00:54.240 --> 00:00:59.080
I have absolutely no idea. I have been on YouTube and

00:00:59.080 --> 00:01:03.880
podcast quite a few times. So much of what I've said is has

00:01:03.880 --> 00:01:07.560
been said many times before and heard many times before.

00:01:08.000 --> 00:01:11.040
And a lot of my friends actually say, oh God, not him again.

00:01:12.360 --> 00:01:14.960
Because yeah, it's all been said and heard before.

00:01:14.960 --> 00:01:17.200
But it's, it's good to be here with you.

00:01:17.200 --> 00:01:18.880
It's been a while actually since I've done.

00:01:19.280 --> 00:01:21.720
You know, as I was researching for the show, I was trying to,

00:01:21.720 --> 00:01:24.280
uh, I mean, of course I've binged on every single piece of

00:01:24.280 --> 00:01:27.040
information that you've put out there, but I was looking up and

00:01:27.040 --> 00:01:29.800
these days search engines are switching to AI and everything

00:01:29.800 --> 00:01:32.360
is like you, you talk to the bots and they respond.

00:01:32.800 --> 00:01:36.080
And I was like, the amount of information that Russ has put

00:01:36.080 --> 00:01:40.080
out through various mediums, It's like there can be a single

00:01:40.080 --> 00:01:43.960
section like Russ Ogden Paragliding Encyclopaedia itself

00:01:44.400 --> 00:01:48.080
with the with this knowledge that kind of has been generated

00:01:48.080 --> 00:01:49.840
over the past years or so. Yeah.

00:01:49.840 --> 00:01:52.080
Well, I think it's really important that we, you know,

00:01:52.080 --> 00:01:54.480
knowledge is key in this sport. And I think it's really

00:01:54.480 --> 00:02:00.400
important that there's a there's a free spread of information to

00:02:00.400 --> 00:02:04.200
to make the sport as so that we all have as much information as

00:02:04.200 --> 00:02:06.120
possible. It's so, so important.

00:02:06.520 --> 00:02:12.080
And especially when we're at the sharp end sort of working as and

00:02:12.160 --> 00:02:14.920
it's, I'm happy and glad to be able to share it.

00:02:14.920 --> 00:02:18.960
And I, I always classify myself as second generation pilot.

00:02:20.040 --> 00:02:23.160
By the time I started flying, there was an established

00:02:23.160 --> 00:02:29.720
training regime in the UK. The BHPA had had a complete

00:02:30.000 --> 00:02:36.040
training schedule. And so I was very much part of

00:02:36.200 --> 00:02:39.680
the second generation and I and when I started, I looked up to

00:02:39.680 --> 00:02:43.000
the first generation pilots, the guys that were flying the 9

00:02:43.000 --> 00:02:48.120
cells and slowly evolving to more higher performance wings.

00:02:48.120 --> 00:02:52.760
But by the time I first started flying, we already had high

00:02:52.760 --> 00:02:56.320
performing paragliders that look like paragliders we fly today

00:02:56.320 --> 00:03:00.360
rather than old parachutes. So I think I got into the sport

00:03:00.360 --> 00:03:03.120
at just the right time. The point that I was trying to

00:03:03.120 --> 00:03:06.920
get to was that I do remember you, you speaking in one of your

00:03:06.920 --> 00:03:11.680
shows that you knew from day one that this was for you.

00:03:12.320 --> 00:03:17.480
What I'm wondering is that did you kind of envision a life of

00:03:18.080 --> 00:03:22.520
where you are at now that you'll be spearheading, let's say the

00:03:22.520 --> 00:03:25.760
R&D and one of the foremost players in the industry

00:03:26.040 --> 00:03:29.200
impacting so many other paragliding pilots lives by that

00:03:29.480 --> 00:03:34.640
and of course, you know, sharing the joy with the community and

00:03:34.640 --> 00:03:38.400
everybody else around you? No, I didn't aspire to do that

00:03:38.400 --> 00:03:41.000
and I'm not sure if I even do that right now.

00:03:41.000 --> 00:03:45.240
But I was teaching paragliding for 10 years and I really

00:03:45.240 --> 00:03:49.920
enjoyed teaching paragliding and I thought that's probably what I

00:03:49.920 --> 00:03:54.680
would remain doing. But now I work, you know, as, as

00:03:54.680 --> 00:03:58.840
a test pilot for ozone. Just a very small role, a small

00:03:58.840 --> 00:04:05.560
part in a in a big machine that is ozone and I, I play but a

00:04:05.560 --> 00:04:10.800
small role in that. One thing that I've kind of seen

00:04:10.800 --> 00:04:13.640
from you every time like you have interacted with someone is

00:04:13.640 --> 00:04:15.960
that you, you come across as a very humble person.

00:04:17.399 --> 00:04:21.279
Is there something that you have kind of done right, which you

00:04:21.279 --> 00:04:24.560
have noticed, of course, besides your personality and, you know,

00:04:24.560 --> 00:04:29.640
just being humble in itself that is kind of made you that way,

00:04:29.640 --> 00:04:33.280
But still, is there something that helped you stay like that

00:04:33.280 --> 00:04:37.040
and kind of come across as an approachable and easy to talk

00:04:37.040 --> 00:04:41.080
to? No, I just, I, I guess I think

00:04:41.440 --> 00:04:45.640
humility is a British value and also I've never seen it as I've

00:04:45.640 --> 00:04:48.720
never seen myself as anything special in the world's of, of

00:04:48.720 --> 00:04:53.160
pilots. Everything I've achieved and,

00:04:53.160 --> 00:04:58.400
and acquired is by hard work and practice and a lot of steady

00:04:58.400 --> 00:05:01.200
progression. I, I, I was, you know, it took,

00:05:01.640 --> 00:05:07.240
it took years to, to kind of get to any reasonable level for me.

00:05:07.240 --> 00:05:10.880
I wasn't, I wasn't necessarily the fastest learner or the OR,

00:05:11.280 --> 00:05:14.480
or progressed so fast. But back then there wasn't the

00:05:14.480 --> 00:05:17.160
information. It was really difficult to, you

00:05:17.160 --> 00:05:21.640
know, progress fast because everything we were doing was, we

00:05:21.640 --> 00:05:25.120
were learning as we went. You know, we didn't have him.

00:05:25.400 --> 00:05:29.760
Nowadays you see the kids come along 1617 and they're doing

00:05:29.760 --> 00:05:32.560
infinite tumbles and all of these things and, and they've

00:05:32.560 --> 00:05:36.720
only been flying a few years. And when I look back at how it

00:05:36.720 --> 00:05:39.040
was in the 90s, I mean, there was no charge.

00:05:39.040 --> 00:05:41.760
We were, we were doing wing overs, you know, and even then

00:05:41.760 --> 00:05:46.840
they were radical and, and the wings we had, the equipment we

00:05:46.840 --> 00:05:50.520
had wasn't quite as up to the standards that they have now.

00:05:51.880 --> 00:05:54.320
But I think, I think knowledge is key there.

00:05:54.320 --> 00:05:59.120
And nowadays you can, you can get, there's so much sources of

00:05:59.120 --> 00:06:04.320
information and there's so many good instructors that can guide

00:06:04.520 --> 00:06:06.720
pilots through. I mean, we had good instructors

00:06:06.720 --> 00:06:10.400
back in the 90s that knew their stuff, but it was the blind

00:06:10.400 --> 00:06:15.280
leading the blind because the, the, the, the local sky gods had

00:06:15.280 --> 00:06:18.080
100 hours, you know, and 100 hours, it's like, my God,

00:06:18.080 --> 00:06:22.280
they're so experienced. Whereas nowadays 100 hours

00:06:22.280 --> 00:06:25.040
doesn't really mean a great deal.

00:06:26.080 --> 00:06:27.560
So yeah, we've progressed a long way.

00:06:28.320 --> 00:06:32.640
Yeah, I mean hard work does kind of shape your personality up to

00:06:32.640 --> 00:06:35.520
a point as well. But like the the reason why I

00:06:35.520 --> 00:06:39.000
asked that question is I want to know a little bit of ideology of

00:06:39.000 --> 00:06:44.320
gratitude because gratitude can mitigate a lot of lot of, I

00:06:44.320 --> 00:06:46.960
wouldn't say negative feelings, but like feelings which you

00:06:46.960 --> 00:06:48.640
don't want to have at a certain stage.

00:06:49.080 --> 00:06:54.480
And I think maybe listeners can resonate to this, that quite a

00:06:54.480 --> 00:06:59.080
few times when you land from a flight which hasn't gone the way

00:06:59.080 --> 00:07:04.760
you wanted it to be, you are not really in the right mindset.

00:07:05.480 --> 00:07:10.400
Regardless of the the fact that you are one of the lucky few who

00:07:10.440 --> 00:07:14.600
get to fly a machine called Paraglider in the world out of

00:07:14.600 --> 00:07:16.680
everyone else, you know that's here on the planet.

00:07:17.640 --> 00:07:20.960
And this can be applied in cross country context or even

00:07:20.960 --> 00:07:23.480
especially in competition if you bomb out.

00:07:23.480 --> 00:07:27.840
Or you know, when you compare yourself as humans to others and

00:07:27.840 --> 00:07:31.080
comparison is the root of all evil, but somehow we all end up

00:07:31.080 --> 00:07:36.480
doing it. So how do you navigate the

00:07:36.480 --> 00:07:40.640
formal or feelings which come with those kind of after

00:07:41.240 --> 00:07:44.720
effects? I think that's the the hardest

00:07:45.000 --> 00:07:47.720
part to deal with really is disappointment in paradigm

00:07:47.720 --> 00:07:49.800
competitions. But I think it's the same in any

00:07:49.800 --> 00:07:53.640
sport, if any, any sport that you do professionally.

00:07:54.640 --> 00:08:01.640
I think, you know, learning to lose, learning to learning to

00:08:01.640 --> 00:08:05.840
not achieve what you expect and being able to deal with that is

00:08:05.840 --> 00:08:09.560
a really important process because that's how you learn and

00:08:09.560 --> 00:08:13.960
how you grow. And paragliding is a whole world

00:08:13.960 --> 00:08:19.040
of paragliding competitions. Is, is is mostly disappointment.

00:08:19.960 --> 00:08:22.960
And if you don't deal with that disappointment very well, then

00:08:22.960 --> 00:08:25.360
you you don't last very long as a competition pilot.

00:08:25.840 --> 00:08:29.000
And from when I first started flying, I knew I was in it for

00:08:29.000 --> 00:08:32.360
the long run. So I was not really, I wasn't

00:08:32.760 --> 00:08:37.880
aiming to for a quick fix. I I wanted to be able to do this

00:08:37.880 --> 00:08:42.480
for my adult life and, and, and get enjoyment from it.

00:08:42.919 --> 00:08:48.560
And, and I'm learning to deal with your defeats is just

00:08:48.720 --> 00:08:51.120
crucial, just crucial to paragliding.

00:08:51.440 --> 00:08:54.720
I grew up playing quite a bit of golf and I still play golf and

00:08:54.720 --> 00:08:57.120
that's very much a a similar sport.

00:08:57.120 --> 00:09:01.000
I love golf, but I, I know I've, but I've never walked away from

00:09:01.000 --> 00:09:03.080
a golf course thinking that was a brilliant.

00:09:03.080 --> 00:09:05.760
I've always walked away thinking that was a bit disappointing

00:09:05.760 --> 00:09:08.080
because I missed this and I missed that and and so on.

00:09:08.080 --> 00:09:11.680
So golf was a good level for me and a good a good sport to kind

00:09:11.680 --> 00:09:15.520
of give you the right mindset to, to to compete with.

00:09:16.640 --> 00:09:20.640
I I read somewhere that you have to let emotions come as well,

00:09:20.640 --> 00:09:22.080
and that is a part of being human.

00:09:22.560 --> 00:09:27.080
And I'd like to take the example of, let's say Roger Federer is

00:09:27.080 --> 00:09:32.520
one of the all time greats in tennis and Maxim Pino is on,

00:09:32.520 --> 00:09:35.000
like you can say on a similar level, the way he chases

00:09:35.000 --> 00:09:39.400
excellence in our sport. Both of them, the reason why I'm

00:09:39.400 --> 00:09:42.720
comparing them right now is that Roger Federer said that there

00:09:42.720 --> 00:09:46.440
was a stage in life when he was quite used to throwing away the

00:09:46.440 --> 00:09:49.920
rackets and the tantrums on a coat until one day he just

00:09:49.920 --> 00:09:52.880
decided that enough is enough and he has to change his

00:09:52.880 --> 00:09:54.920
personality because he's throwing away his matches.

00:09:56.400 --> 00:09:58.800
I remember speaking to Maxime because he has been on the show

00:09:58.800 --> 00:10:02.960
as well earlier. And I think this was PwC

00:10:02.960 --> 00:10:07.960
Krochevo last year when he bombed out in day two.

00:10:08.120 --> 00:10:11.480
He was leading as usual. And then he probably went too

00:10:11.480 --> 00:10:15.240
aggressive and then took the first retrieve bus back to the

00:10:15.240 --> 00:10:17.480
hotel. And I asked him, I was like, are

00:10:17.480 --> 00:10:20.320
you dealing with it fine? And he was like, yeah, yeah, I

00:10:20.720 --> 00:10:22.440
was. And I said how?

00:10:22.880 --> 00:10:25.440
And like, what's, what's your thought process?

00:10:25.440 --> 00:10:29.040
And he said, like, I have thrown quite a few helmets in my back

00:10:29.040 --> 00:10:31.840
in the day, but now I've kind of gotten over it.

00:10:32.080 --> 00:10:36.640
So is it more or less like a process that you cannot force of

00:10:36.640 --> 00:10:39.200
calming down and learning to deal with your defeats?

00:10:39.200 --> 00:10:43.440
Or is it a conscious effort that the sooner you do, the better it

00:10:43.440 --> 00:10:45.520
is? Because in the end, you're the

00:10:45.520 --> 00:10:47.960
only one who's entering a self destruct mode when you do that.

00:10:48.240 --> 00:10:51.320
Nobody else is paying the price for your happiness or for your

00:10:51.320 --> 00:10:54.160
defeats. Yeah, for me, it's a conscious

00:10:54.160 --> 00:10:55.160
effort. Yeah, totally.

00:10:55.160 --> 00:10:56.520
I mean, we've all, we've all done it.

00:10:56.520 --> 00:11:00.880
I mean, I remember the the one time after, you know, I think it

00:11:00.880 --> 00:11:05.000
was my first World Championships many years ago.

00:11:05.000 --> 00:11:07.360
And I've actually flown my personal best.

00:11:07.360 --> 00:11:10.280
I've flown 150 something kilometres, but I only just

00:11:10.280 --> 00:11:14.320
short of the goal and my gloves went in One Direction, the other

00:11:14.320 --> 00:11:17.560
glove in another direction, helmet thrown one way.

00:11:18.680 --> 00:11:21.760
And, and it was that moment where in that landing field

00:11:21.760 --> 00:11:23.880
where I had a word with myself and I thought, come on, Russ,

00:11:23.880 --> 00:11:25.120
what's that? You know, you've just done the

00:11:25.120 --> 00:11:29.200
best flight, the furthest flight you've ever done and you're

00:11:29.200 --> 00:11:31.320
still in one piece. You're an amazing place.

00:11:31.760 --> 00:11:34.200
You're, you're privileged, like you said earlier, you know,

00:11:34.200 --> 00:11:36.440
we're very privileged to be able to do what we do.

00:11:36.440 --> 00:11:42.080
And I realised I was making a big scene over absolutely

00:11:42.200 --> 00:11:46.800
nothing. And so since that day, I, I, I

00:11:46.800 --> 00:11:49.200
have never thrown another helmet or glove anywhere.

00:11:49.720 --> 00:11:52.960
But you still get, I hate bombing out.

00:11:52.960 --> 00:11:56.280
We have to hate bombing out. If you hate bombing out, when

00:11:56.280 --> 00:12:02.440
you, you learn strategies and decision processes that avoid

00:12:02.440 --> 00:12:05.160
bombing out. But I still do it.

00:12:05.240 --> 00:12:10.680
I, I, I bombed out badly last year in, in Macedonia, in

00:12:10.680 --> 00:12:14.080
Krishova as well on the last day of a comp, which I think I was

00:12:14.200 --> 00:12:17.040
winning on the last day or I was close to the top anyway

00:12:17.040 --> 00:12:19.600
somewhere. And I completely blew the comp.

00:12:21.560 --> 00:12:25.120
But you know, I allow myself a couple of minutes to be pissed

00:12:25.120 --> 00:12:29.600
off and then there's no point in carrying on with that mindset.

00:12:29.600 --> 00:12:32.720
So I just let it go. You just have to let it go.

00:12:32.800 --> 00:12:36.800
And I think once you've heard enough disappointment in life,

00:12:36.800 --> 00:12:42.360
you can let it go much easier. I think as you get older and

00:12:42.360 --> 00:12:45.320
more mature as a pilot, not necessarily just in age, but

00:12:45.320 --> 00:12:49.000
just in whatever you're doing, you've, you've had it, you've

00:12:49.000 --> 00:12:51.880
had bad, you know, we've all bombed out and we'll all bombed

00:12:51.880 --> 00:12:55.280
out again in the future. And as long as you have that

00:12:55.280 --> 00:12:58.840
mindset and think of the long run rather than just a very

00:12:58.840 --> 00:13:05.480
short run, then it allows you to kind of deal with it better and

00:13:05.480 --> 00:13:09.120
I think you'll find the best pilots in the world deal with it

00:13:09.120 --> 00:13:14.120
quite well. Does it come to some, like

00:13:14.120 --> 00:13:17.080
somewhere to the levels of testosterone we have in our

00:13:17.080 --> 00:13:19.320
body? I mean, I cannot speak the same

00:13:19.320 --> 00:13:23.960
for female pilots, but as you age it does gradually decrease.

00:13:23.960 --> 00:13:27.120
And of course your drive, your anger, your a lot of other

00:13:27.120 --> 00:13:29.600
things change physically in human body.

00:13:29.880 --> 00:13:32.840
So does it have a bigger role to play as well?

00:13:33.760 --> 00:13:36.120
I think it probably does. I think that, you know, the hot

00:13:36.120 --> 00:13:41.160
headed ones of normally then their 20s, only 20s and they,

00:13:41.240 --> 00:13:43.480
you know, they want to, they want to come, they want to come

00:13:43.480 --> 00:13:47.720
onto the scene and prove a point and, and the, and the the pain

00:13:47.720 --> 00:13:50.440
of not achieving is, is is harder for them.

00:13:50.440 --> 00:13:52.880
I think, you know, like you're talking about Maxine was a bit

00:13:52.880 --> 00:13:56.800
that way when he was younger, Otto was, but now they've been

00:13:56.800 --> 00:14:01.360
through that process, They've won, they've lost, they've won

00:14:01.360 --> 00:14:04.440
again, they've lost again. They've been through this cycle.

00:14:04.440 --> 00:14:08.760
And at the end of the day, it doesn't, it doesn't really

00:14:08.800 --> 00:14:12.800
matter. And I think as long as you, you,

00:14:12.840 --> 00:14:15.720
you keep that in the front of your mind, but it, it doesn't

00:14:15.720 --> 00:14:17.480
really matter. You know, as long as you land

00:14:17.480 --> 00:14:22.360
safely and you're not broken, then it's not so bad.

00:14:22.440 --> 00:14:26.280
And so I think a bit of maturity certainly helps with that.

00:14:26.280 --> 00:14:31.760
I think about testosterone, but I think maturity is, is the is

00:14:31.760 --> 00:14:34.960
what helps deal with that because I think you have to be

00:14:34.960 --> 00:14:41.000
competitive to, to do well. But if you're too competitive,

00:14:41.120 --> 00:14:43.600
you're going out too quickly. And those sort of pilots, they,

00:14:43.600 --> 00:14:47.160
I've seen them come and go, but they don't really stick around a

00:14:47.160 --> 00:14:48.840
huge amount of time, I don't think.

00:14:50.280 --> 00:14:54.760
Please correct me if I'm wrong, but I do remember a statement.

00:14:55.000 --> 00:14:58.080
I don't know where I heard it, but someone said that you

00:14:58.080 --> 00:15:03.440
mentioned paraglider pilots tend to peak around the age of 30

00:15:03.440 --> 00:15:08.560
years in their lifetime, let's say.

00:15:08.560 --> 00:15:11.840
Is that more or less the sweet spot when you have enough

00:15:11.840 --> 00:15:16.880
aggression slash experience and you know you're able to navigate

00:15:17.320 --> 00:15:20.440
the risk versus reward spectrum responsibly?

00:15:20.760 --> 00:15:24.680
Or is there like more to that statement which people don't

00:15:24.680 --> 00:15:29.480
know yet? I think, I think it's to do with

00:15:29.480 --> 00:15:32.880
experience. I think experience is, is just

00:15:33.200 --> 00:15:36.280
the king in, in competitive paragliding.

00:15:37.040 --> 00:15:41.120
We see young guys come along and I'd take Oh no.

00:15:41.160 --> 00:15:43.440
And, and Maxime is a great example.

00:15:43.440 --> 00:15:47.440
They they came on the scene at roughly the same time and they

00:15:47.440 --> 00:15:50.280
were roughly the same age. I think they were early 20s when

00:15:50.280 --> 00:15:55.520
