it looks like it's the latest story on the market and stuff

00:48:51.480 --> 00:48:54.360
like that. So how does how does spending

00:48:54.440 --> 00:48:57.080
make you feel better? Or does it actually make you fly

00:48:57.120 --> 00:49:02.120
better? Yeah, I think, I think it's, I

00:49:02.120 --> 00:49:05.920
think, I think we've seen so much progression in paragliding

00:49:05.920 --> 00:49:11.320
equipment over the last few years, well over the last 15

00:49:11.320 --> 00:49:15.200
years. I would say that there were real

00:49:15.200 --> 00:49:20.600
tangible benefits for upgrading your equipment to get a small

00:49:20.600 --> 00:49:23.880
percentage of additional performance because a small

00:49:23.880 --> 00:49:28.440
percentage of additional performance can actually give

00:49:28.560 --> 00:49:34.960
large amounts of it opens the world to a lot more

00:49:35.280 --> 00:49:38.360
possibilities with flying, you know, just a few percentage more

00:49:38.360 --> 00:49:44.000
performance in your wing can get exponentially more when you're

00:49:44.000 --> 00:49:48.080
trying to fly across country. When I left, I have a similar

00:49:48.080 --> 00:49:52.240
experience to you because I came from a third world country, a

00:49:52.240 --> 00:49:54.920
developing country into into the first world.

00:49:55.120 --> 00:50:01.400
When I left the UK and and moved to France and when I left the UK

00:50:01.440 --> 00:50:07.760
we had the the 100 kilometre club was quite exclusive.

00:50:08.280 --> 00:50:14.200
And now that same club, now the exclusive club is the 300

00:50:14.200 --> 00:50:18.560
kilometre club. And we haven't improved

00:50:18.560 --> 00:50:23.120
performance free fold. We've only added, you know, a

00:50:23.320 --> 00:50:26.280
certain percentage, a small percentage of performance over

00:50:26.280 --> 00:50:31.400
the years, but that just makes flying long distance way more

00:50:31.400 --> 00:50:36.720
feasible and possible. So I think if you can fly a

00:50:37.760 --> 00:50:42.840
modern wing and when I say modern something within six

00:50:42.840 --> 00:50:49.080
years old, you are going to have access to, to pretty top notch

00:50:49.080 --> 00:50:52.600
performance in paragliding harness wise.

00:50:53.040 --> 00:50:56.800
I think you know, the submarine type harness do give additional

00:50:56.800 --> 00:51:00.160
performance, but they're certainly really relevant to

00:51:00.320 --> 00:51:03.640
competition flying or serious cross country flying.

00:51:03.920 --> 00:51:08.640
You can still bang out a 300 kilometre flight on a any

00:51:08.640 --> 00:51:11.640
standard called harness. You don't need anything special

00:51:11.640 --> 00:51:15.320
like that. I think what's different with

00:51:15.320 --> 00:51:18.800
with paragliders is that they, they can't, they, they do age

00:51:18.800 --> 00:51:23.560
obviously and the performance and their safety goes down with

00:51:23.560 --> 00:51:28.120
age. And so there's no reason.

00:51:28.520 --> 00:51:31.840
I mean, I totally understand it in certain places where it's

00:51:31.840 --> 00:51:36.440
financially more restrictive, but there's no point in flying

00:51:36.440 --> 00:51:39.240
wings until they're they become unworthy.

00:51:39.280 --> 00:51:41.360
There's just no point in doing that.

00:51:42.400 --> 00:51:49.680
You may as well just keep good modern equipment that that will

00:51:49.680 --> 00:51:52.680
keep you safe. I'm so glad you mentioned the

00:51:52.840 --> 00:51:55.920
the submarine example because I've been very curious about one

00:51:55.920 --> 00:51:58.400
thing. I'm not sure who I was having a

00:51:58.400 --> 00:52:03.200
chat with but I think when you guys came out with the early

00:52:03.200 --> 00:52:09.680
protos of submarine I heard the story that Honoren was smoking

00:52:09.680 --> 00:52:15.760
people on enzos and conventional harness when he was flying a

00:52:15.760 --> 00:52:20.720
Zeno plus a sub combination. So of course the pilot's skill

00:52:20.720 --> 00:52:24.040
were, you know, top notch and yet he was able to squeeze in

00:52:24.040 --> 00:52:27.760
every last bit of performance from a Xeno, which was eclipsing

00:52:27.760 --> 00:52:30.880
the Enzo's. So when I heard that I was like,

00:52:31.480 --> 00:52:34.800
of course, just like any other pilot, I was very keen to

00:52:34.800 --> 00:52:37.840
upgrade to the flashiest tech available on the market.

00:52:39.120 --> 00:52:45.480
But since it came with a bit of a trade off in back protection,

00:52:46.280 --> 00:52:49.960
I stuck to the XO seat type harness that I fly with.

00:52:51.920 --> 00:52:56.720
Does upgrading for minimum performance gain really make a

00:52:56.720 --> 00:53:02.080
difference when you are not landing within the 1st 25 to 50

00:53:02.080 --> 00:53:05.600
pilots in a PwC? Or is it just a psychological

00:53:05.600 --> 00:53:07.960
thing that everyone is flying the flashiest twice so I have to

00:53:07.960 --> 00:53:12.880
be there as well? No, I think it's, I think it's

00:53:13.720 --> 00:53:17.080
not really of value. I, I, I see very good pilots who

00:53:17.080 --> 00:53:20.560
don't fly submarines doing very well in competitions.

00:53:22.560 --> 00:53:26.120
The speed and efficiency is borne by decision making.

00:53:26.120 --> 00:53:29.240
It's not borne by the equipment that you use.

00:53:30.200 --> 00:53:33.280
You could go to an any average competition.

00:53:33.280 --> 00:53:37.800
Powell Takax kind of proved that point in the SRS when he won

00:53:38.360 --> 00:53:44.840
flying a rush, an E&B wing. And so no, your speed and

00:53:44.840 --> 00:53:48.040
efficiency is borne by decisions, not the equipment you

00:53:48.040 --> 00:53:51.680
fly. For sure if you want to, if you

00:53:51.680 --> 00:53:54.360
want to compete at the top level, then obviously you need

00:53:54.360 --> 00:53:59.200
to be on on the on the right equipment and have it set

00:53:59.200 --> 00:54:01.920
correctly. But if you just want to if you

00:54:01.920 --> 00:54:05.160
want to roll up to APWC and finish in the top 20, you don't

00:54:05.160 --> 00:54:07.520
have to do that. No, you have just have to make

00:54:07.600 --> 00:54:13.040
good decisions and fly well throughout the week for a top 20

00:54:13.040 --> 00:54:14.680
finish. If you want to start getting

00:54:14.680 --> 00:54:17.400
near the podium or top ten then maybe you need the best.

00:54:17.400 --> 00:54:20.760
But no you can. You can finish very well in the

00:54:20.760 --> 00:54:23.760
PwC on a Zeno and a non submarine heart.

00:54:24.800 --> 00:54:28.440
Damn that's so nice to hear because I think a lot of people

00:54:28.440 --> 00:54:32.200
love the marketing gimmick and up to a point I think in in a

00:54:32.760 --> 00:54:34.720
capitalist society it does mess with your head.

00:54:34.720 --> 00:54:38.240
But that is where self-control and everything else comes, that

00:54:38.240 --> 00:54:41.080
you face the reality rather than just getting carried away and

00:54:41.080 --> 00:54:44.440
then make responsible decisions. Yeah, well, it is exciting as

00:54:44.440 --> 00:54:46.480
well, isn't it? It's exciting to have new

00:54:46.480 --> 00:54:48.440
equipment. It's exciting to have the best

00:54:48.440 --> 00:54:50.760
equipment. It's exciting and it's great.

00:54:50.760 --> 00:54:53.560
It's great. We're in a position now when I

00:54:53.560 --> 00:54:56.200
first started competing, you couldn't compete against the

00:54:56.200 --> 00:54:58.800
factory pilots because they had the prototypes, they had the

00:54:58.800 --> 00:55:01.640
special equipment that wasn't accessible to all.

00:55:01.840 --> 00:55:05.760
One of the beautiful things that's happened in our sport in

00:55:05.760 --> 00:55:11.480
the last 15 years, really, yeah, 15 years, is that you can.

00:55:11.480 --> 00:55:15.720
Now we have access to all the same equipment that the factory

00:55:15.720 --> 00:55:20.520
pilots have and that's levelled the playing field somewhat and

00:55:21.680 --> 00:55:26.960
and made it possible for any dedicated experienced pilot to

00:55:26.960 --> 00:55:29.640
compete at the top level, at the best level.

00:55:30.520 --> 00:55:34.680
I was actually going to come to the manufacturer's point anyway.

00:55:34.720 --> 00:55:38.360
So I'm glad we are talking about this Like paragliding is an

00:55:38.360 --> 00:55:42.960
industry that has evolved. I would, I would say rapidly up

00:55:42.960 --> 00:55:46.800
to a point, you know, like free flight has kind of gone on in

00:55:47.000 --> 00:55:50.280
leaps and bounds when it comes to convenience and performance.

00:55:50.280 --> 00:55:55.480
And you know, putting all those parameters and any, any fast

00:55:55.480 --> 00:56:00.280
growing industry requires everyone to keep up pace to

00:56:00.280 --> 00:56:03.520
survive. A lot of times we have seen

00:56:03.520 --> 00:56:07.960
niche industries are more or less like one man, one man LED

00:56:07.960 --> 00:56:11.680
army where the the inventors, the founders are the ones who

00:56:11.680 --> 00:56:15.720
are spearheading and as soon as they start fading, the the

00:56:15.720 --> 00:56:19.760
creation kind of starts to fade with them.

00:56:20.960 --> 00:56:22.880
A lot of brands have come and gone.

00:56:23.040 --> 00:56:26.760
A lot of good ones have come, done their bit and moved on.

00:56:27.280 --> 00:56:32.000
But legacy brands are there for a reason.

00:56:32.000 --> 00:56:36.240
And Ozone has remained, I wouldn't just say relevant, but

00:56:36.240 --> 00:56:41.120
like at the forefront of pushing the limits, setting the new

00:56:41.120 --> 00:56:44.640
state of school and redefining what's possible next.

00:56:45.640 --> 00:56:50.040
Is there a short answer to how did you guys do it?

00:56:50.920 --> 00:56:53.520
Like if there's an aspiring brand listening to this.

00:56:55.480 --> 00:57:00.920
I think, I think it's a combination of the work ethic

00:57:00.920 --> 00:57:05.400
that we have in ozone. Ozone from from early when I

00:57:05.400 --> 00:57:07.640
first started, the work ethic was very strong.

00:57:07.640 --> 00:57:13.000
We had very high standards for ourselves and we always were

00:57:13.000 --> 00:57:16.800
looking to to make the best things that we possibly can.

00:57:19.000 --> 00:57:24.920
But I think the major, the major force behind the our success in

00:57:24.920 --> 00:57:29.120
competitions is Luke Carmel. We, when, when Luke joined the,

00:57:29.120 --> 00:57:34.480
the company, he bought a level of knowledge and understanding

00:57:36.440 --> 00:57:44.160
and a thought process that has allowed us to maintain that

00:57:44.880 --> 00:57:50.440
level at the top. And so I think there's, I think

00:57:50.440 --> 00:57:54.760
we have a lot of thanks for the, for the work that Luke has done

00:57:54.840 --> 00:57:59.200
and the, the, the, the ethic and the, the way we think about

00:57:59.840 --> 00:58:03.680
processing the thoughts of progression is, is very much

00:58:03.680 --> 00:58:05.920
down to him. But he's been backed.

00:58:05.920 --> 00:58:09.600
What, what, what what's been good with Ozone is that, you

00:58:09.600 --> 00:58:14.160
know, especially when in the early days, he was backed by

00:58:14.160 --> 00:58:19.840
design by David, who had experience, and also by the

00:58:19.840 --> 00:58:25.440
bosses of Ozone who almost gave free reign and, you know, the

00:58:25.440 --> 00:58:29.160
ability to, yeah, go try this, do that, try that, do that.

00:58:29.160 --> 00:58:32.160
We weren't held back by from above.

00:58:32.960 --> 00:58:37.920
So it's, it's a little bit different now because the cost

00:58:37.920 --> 00:58:40.880
of everything, the cost of prototypes has gone up so much

00:58:40.880 --> 00:58:44.200
that we are not, we're not quite as free rein as we once were,

00:58:45.120 --> 00:58:48.880
but we've, we've certainly kept the philosophy that it's always

00:58:48.880 --> 00:58:51.880
good to innovate. We're, you know, we've, we're,

00:58:51.880 --> 00:58:57.200
we're an R&D company and we aim to keep innovating and we've

00:58:57.200 --> 00:59:00.240
innovative in, in different ways in different areas.

00:59:00.240 --> 00:59:03.840
You know, Luke, Luke gave us the, the competition side, the

00:59:03.840 --> 00:59:08.760
high performance side. But we've also we're very proud

00:59:08.760 --> 00:59:11.240
of the, the work we've done in the lightweight equipment.

00:59:11.240 --> 00:59:14.520
For example, you know when and that goes back from when David

00:59:14.520 --> 00:59:18.040
first did the X Alps, we made a high performance lightweight

00:59:18.040 --> 00:59:24.160
wing and from when we made the ultra light series down to the

00:59:24.160 --> 00:59:26.720
extra extra light, which is where we made the first single

00:59:26.720 --> 00:59:32.640
surface wing. So NF lights harness that was

00:59:32.720 --> 00:59:37.560
under was 100 grams. So we, we, we concentrate on

00:59:37.560 --> 00:59:43.680
being first and foremost an R&B company and we invest a lot in

00:59:44.600 --> 00:59:47.920
the team, the R&D team. We've we've enlarged recently,

00:59:47.920 --> 00:59:54.520
we've taken on more brain power. And so there's a good design

00:59:54.520 --> 00:59:57.280
base of engineers working for Ozone right now.

00:59:57.560 --> 01:00:02.280
And much of this focus recently is going into harnesses, and we

01:00:02.280 --> 01:00:05.640
will see better harnesses in the future as well.

01:00:06.760 --> 01:00:12.440
I'm very curious about your founding members because like

01:00:12.440 --> 01:00:15.720
Robbie Whittle only has, you know, so much like Internet

01:00:15.720 --> 01:00:20.720
presence. But every time he speaks, I I

01:00:20.720 --> 01:00:24.760
feel like there's a lot of passion behind his thoughts.

01:00:24.760 --> 01:00:27.720
And, you know, the way he conveys the way he talks about

01:00:27.720 --> 01:00:30.360
like it's, it's just fascinating to hear him talk about flying.

01:00:31.040 --> 01:00:33.840
And I really wish to have him on the show as well, because that

01:00:33.840 --> 01:00:36.960
is like beyond words, something that I've envisioned as well,

01:00:36.960 --> 01:00:39.640
just like you. But I'm, I'm just wondering,

01:00:40.680 --> 01:00:44.400
monetizing your passion is, I think, the biggest blessing that

01:00:44.400 --> 01:00:48.880
exists on this planet because somehow you are able to make

01:00:48.880 --> 01:00:53.040
sense of your reality and be happy with it and always have

01:00:53.040 --> 01:00:56.560
something to look forward to. Do you have some advice for

01:00:56.560 --> 01:00:59.920
people who are looking to monetize their passion in this

01:00:59.920 --> 01:01:03.360
industry, considering that you you are a part of one of the

01:01:03.360 --> 01:01:05.480
most successful teams that exists on this planet?

01:01:07.680 --> 01:01:12.160
I think. I think the one fundamental

01:01:12.160 --> 01:01:18.040
thing you have to have is just a deep love for, for flying.

01:01:19.680 --> 01:01:26.240
And that deep love changes as you, as, as you, as you grow and

01:01:26.240 --> 01:01:28.560
as you age. And it, you know, you go, I've

01:01:28.560 --> 01:01:30.920
talked about it before in the past where it's, it's like a

01:01:30.920 --> 01:01:32.920
marriage. You know, you have this lustful

01:01:33.360 --> 01:01:37.760
first few years and then it fades and, and then it turns

01:01:37.760 --> 01:01:41.520
into a deeper love. So I, I, I see.

01:01:41.520 --> 01:01:44.560
Well, I always go back to my marriage analogy with, with

01:01:44.560 --> 01:01:46.520
flights. It's certainly been that way for

01:01:46.520 --> 01:01:50.640
me. And and Rob's a good example

01:01:50.640 --> 01:01:53.560
that you that you bring up. When I joined Ozone, the reason

01:01:53.560 --> 01:01:56.520
I joined Ozone was because Rob wanted to stop testing.

01:01:57.520 --> 01:02:01.680
And he said to me, he said, he said, Russ, yeah, I'm done with

01:02:01.680 --> 01:02:03.120
it. I don't want to test anymore.

01:02:03.120 --> 01:02:06.320
And I remember being at that stage in my flight.

01:02:06.320 --> 01:02:09.600
I've been flying 10 years, but I couldn't empathise with what he

01:02:09.600 --> 01:02:13.000
was saying. I just couldn't understand how

01:02:13.000 --> 01:02:16.640
he could be in a position where he didn't want to continue

01:02:16.640 --> 01:02:20.640
flying or pesting or developing. It's, I couldn't, I couldn't

01:02:20.640 --> 01:02:23.880
relate to it at all. And it's, it's only recently

01:02:23.880 --> 01:02:27.240
that I kind of, yeah, I understand where he's at now.

01:02:27.240 --> 01:02:30.160
Because once you've done it enough and you've scratched your

01:02:30.160 --> 01:02:35.400
itch, you've, you've got rid of that just absolute need to, to

01:02:35.400 --> 01:02:38.600
fly. Yeah, in other factors, other

01:02:38.960 --> 01:02:43.120
things in life become, come to the fore and become more

01:02:43.120 --> 01:02:46.600
important. David de Gaulle, the guy who I

01:02:46.600 --> 01:02:51.560
work with, he, you know, he's an amazing pilot, super talented

01:02:51.600 --> 01:02:57.360
test pilot, designer and who taught me how to test.

01:02:57.520 --> 01:03:03.520
And I worked with him when I first joined Ozone and recently

01:03:03.520 --> 01:03:06.760
he's kind of stopped flying. He stopped testing and and flies

