I have come across so many instructors.

01:06:07.560 --> 01:06:12.640
I don't mean to badmouth anyone, but if you are reducing your own

01:06:12.640 --> 01:06:16.920
quality of imparting what you love the most, then you're doing

01:06:16.920 --> 01:06:20.960
injustice to the system. First of all, how to identify

01:06:21.280 --> 01:06:24.200
that the stage has come, and secondly, how to convince

01:06:24.200 --> 01:06:28.040
yourself that you have to make it happen otherwise you're

01:06:29.480 --> 01:06:31.600
hitting the axe on your own foot kind of a thing.

01:06:32.640 --> 01:06:35.480
Yeah, I mean, it's hard to, it's hard to identify, but we've all

01:06:35.480 --> 01:06:37.720
seen those instructors and we see them regularly.

01:06:37.720 --> 01:06:42.200
You know, they, they've been teaching too long and it's

01:06:42.200 --> 01:06:44.520
become part of their life and what they do.

01:06:44.680 --> 01:06:49.280
But they, they don't really give it the same energy that they

01:06:49.280 --> 01:06:51.760
did, you know, a few years previous.

01:06:52.200 --> 01:06:54.960
I definitely saw it with myself teaching.

01:06:55.000 --> 01:06:59.480
I taught SIV for a long time as well and I could see it

01:06:59.480 --> 01:07:02.720
happening in SIV as well. And it got to a point where it's

01:07:02.720 --> 01:07:06.560
like, OK, you know, that's it's, it's a good time to stop, quit

01:07:06.560 --> 01:07:09.760
while we were ahead. And half's on the batten to

01:07:09.760 --> 01:07:14.880
people who are rated younger and motivated and wanting to, to do

01:07:14.880 --> 01:07:17.640
that. So I think you just have to be

01:07:17.640 --> 01:07:20.800
brutally honest with yourself and also accept that there is a,

01:07:21.160 --> 01:07:24.400
you know, there is a finite time for these things to be at their

01:07:24.400 --> 01:07:29.400
best. But no, it's, it's hard to walk

01:07:29.400 --> 01:07:31.520
away from when you've been doing it for a while.

01:07:31.520 --> 01:07:35.920
Definitely, definitely. And I do still miss teaching SRB

01:07:35.920 --> 01:07:38.680
every now and then. Not a great deal, but a little

01:07:38.680 --> 01:07:43.680
bit. Can the success of Ozone be also

01:07:46.120 --> 01:07:50.000
contributed to the fact that the founding members did eventually

01:07:50.000 --> 01:07:52.000
pass on the button when it was the right time?

01:07:52.240 --> 01:07:54.600
Like in that sense. Oh well.

01:07:54.600 --> 01:07:58.160
But the guys who started the company is still very much there

01:07:58.160 --> 01:08:00.160
and they're still very much involved with it.

01:08:01.480 --> 01:08:08.720
Mike Kavanaugh is the CEO. David Pilkington was the was in

01:08:08.720 --> 01:08:12.000
charge of our production. We have a, we have our own

01:08:12.000 --> 01:08:17.520
factory in Vietnam and he was responsible for for setting all

01:08:17.520 --> 01:08:19.760
that up and getting that things going.

01:08:19.760 --> 01:08:23.080
He lived out there for many, many years before he came back

01:08:23.080 --> 01:08:26.840
to the UK, went back to the UK. So they're still involved with

01:08:26.840 --> 01:08:29.760
the day-to-day running, but they have let go.

01:08:29.800 --> 01:08:34.840
You know, Mike used to be in control of every little bit of

01:08:34.840 --> 01:08:38.479
ozone and now he's let it go. But he could only let that go

01:08:38.479 --> 01:08:41.240
once the company had reached a certain level.

01:08:42.240 --> 01:08:46.680
And now we have a management team and, and it's a very

01:08:46.680 --> 01:08:49.319
different company now than it was when I joined.

01:08:49.319 --> 01:08:52.760
But, but, you know, change is the only certainty in life and

01:08:52.760 --> 01:08:58.040
growth is, is really important. So now we're a, we're a much

01:08:59.479 --> 01:09:01.520
bigger company. There's more of us.

01:09:03.319 --> 01:09:05.560
People do specialist, more specialist roles.

01:09:07.359 --> 01:09:12.399
And change is good. Change is necessary and to stay

01:09:12.399 --> 01:09:17.120
at the top now you need to be innovative and you need to be

01:09:17.120 --> 01:09:19.479
able to make good product. You know, we're only as good as

01:09:19.479 --> 01:09:23.359
our last wing that we make and you can't rest on your laurels.

01:09:23.760 --> 01:09:27.399
We've seen it with other manufacturers come and go and I

01:09:27.399 --> 01:09:32.760
think there was a period, there was a period where it seemed

01:09:32.760 --> 01:09:35.520
like we're seeing it now with harness manufacturers.

01:09:35.520 --> 01:09:38.520
In fact, there's a few harness manufacturers just popping up

01:09:39.080 --> 01:09:42.960
making boutique submarines and things like that.

01:09:42.960 --> 01:09:49.080
And, and I think that's really hard for someone to maintain for

01:09:49.080 --> 01:09:52.920
a long time. Because if you're if you're, if

01:09:52.920 --> 01:09:55.760
you want to start a paragliding company, it's relatively easy.

01:09:55.760 --> 01:09:59.000
You just get some software and you can send off your designs to

01:09:59.000 --> 01:10:01.520
a factory and they can send you prototypes.

01:10:01.760 --> 01:10:06.800
It's not that hard, but the work that goes on behind a product to

01:10:06.800 --> 01:10:11.240
get it to market with the marketing, the photos, the

01:10:11.280 --> 01:10:15.600
websites, the stock management, the order systems, it's, it's,

01:10:15.600 --> 01:10:18.000
it's a, it's a massive endeavour.

01:10:18.000 --> 01:10:22.920
And I think it's becoming more and more difficult to be able to

01:10:22.920 --> 01:10:26.880
do that as a one man show than it was 20 years ago.

01:10:26.880 --> 01:10:32.920
And I think now the the, the industry is maturing in a way

01:10:32.920 --> 01:10:37.880
that there's a lot of good established companies making

01:10:38.800 --> 01:10:40.880
good products right now. Right now we're in a really good

01:10:40.880 --> 01:10:45.720
position in the paragliding world where we've got a choice,

01:10:45.880 --> 01:10:52.120
a big choice of very good wings and harm seasons.

01:10:52.480 --> 01:10:55.360
You know, there's a lot of innovative divide designers out

01:10:55.360 --> 01:10:57.520
there. So we're in a healthy position

01:10:57.520 --> 01:10:59.440
at the moment, I think like the industry as a whole.

01:11:00.360 --> 01:11:04.680
I'm glad you mentioned change is the only constant because that

01:11:04.680 --> 01:11:08.840
kind of I every time I think about evolution of wings as

01:11:08.840 --> 01:11:11.360
well. And I, you know, I'm diverting

01:11:11.360 --> 01:11:17.160
from the topic a bit here, but from open class to triple C and

01:11:17.160 --> 01:11:24.400
now CSC, the civil sports class, there has been noise around the

01:11:24.400 --> 01:11:31.080
change from both, you know, like sides which say this is good,

01:11:31.080 --> 01:11:35.600
this is not. But somehow a lot of times I

01:11:36.120 --> 01:11:41.520
personally feel that when excellence is basically

01:11:41.520 --> 01:11:44.760
limitless, right? That is the way you can excel in

01:11:44.760 --> 01:11:48.200
something when you truly chase it in a limitless manner.

01:11:49.000 --> 01:11:54.560
Are we changing for the good or are we changing just because

01:11:54.560 --> 01:11:58.760
change is the only constant? Very good question, I think.

01:11:59.720 --> 01:12:04.920
I think the whole, the whole process of what we've seen over

01:12:04.920 --> 01:12:08.760
the last years, you know, over the 15 years where we went away

01:12:08.760 --> 01:12:15.520
from open class into CCC and now into sport class competitions,

01:12:15.520 --> 01:12:20.040
CCFC and the whole debate that's gone on recently about

01:12:22.360 --> 01:12:24.040
harnesses, protection and the like.

01:12:25.880 --> 01:12:30.880
For me, it's a line drawing exercise and by which I mean we

01:12:30.880 --> 01:12:35.920
all have a line and my line will possibly be different than yours

01:12:37.320 --> 01:12:40.040
and my line is different than my wife's and my line is very

01:12:40.040 --> 01:12:44.800
different than my mother's. And so where we draw this line,

01:12:44.800 --> 01:12:47.840
where do we say what is acceptable, what is better, what

01:12:47.840 --> 01:12:51.400
is worse? Where we draw that line is.

01:12:52.320 --> 01:12:56.200
And I think the reason, you know, the utterings that's been

01:12:56.200 --> 01:12:59.880
going on in the the pilot union and, and all of that movement

01:13:00.720 --> 01:13:04.760
just shows how we all have slightly different lines.

01:13:05.800 --> 01:13:09.400
Where we decide to draw that line I think is a really

01:13:09.400 --> 01:13:14.040
personal opinion. What is nice is that we're

01:13:14.040 --> 01:13:20.880
creating categories where people can fly comfortably in the

01:13:20.960 --> 01:13:23.640
within the lines that they draw for themselves.

01:13:24.240 --> 01:13:27.520
So I think we're moving in the right direction and we're moving

01:13:28.280 --> 01:13:31.240
for the better. But there's part of me that

01:13:31.240 --> 01:13:35.520
still arcs back a little bit towards the open class days.

01:13:35.520 --> 01:13:42.320
There was, there was a beauty of the open class and, but I think

01:13:42.320 --> 01:13:46.920
the reduction of the top speed for the CCC has, has, has led to

01:13:47.640 --> 01:13:53.680
a real improvement in safety coupled with a big improvement

01:13:53.680 --> 01:13:57.800
in pilot skill as well and pilot understanding.

01:13:58.240 --> 01:14:03.400
I used to, I remember the first few PWC's you'd go to years ago,

01:14:03.480 --> 01:14:06.520
you, you, you'd see someone take a 50% collapse and end up

01:14:06.520 --> 01:14:10.680
throwing their reserve and it all seemed a bit futile and

01:14:10.680 --> 01:14:13.360
pointless. Whereas nowadays I think

01:14:13.360 --> 01:14:16.640
people's base level is much higher blighted control.

01:14:16.640 --> 01:14:19.960
So we see less reserve deployments, our top speed is

01:14:19.960 --> 01:14:22.960
reduced, our gliders are a little bit more stable.

01:14:23.040 --> 01:14:28.000
So I think, I think the last 10 years, the last decade has been,

01:14:28.000 --> 01:14:30.200
has been the heyday of paragliding competitions.

01:14:30.200 --> 01:14:32.760
Right now, paragliding competitions have been more

01:14:32.760 --> 01:14:36.480
popular than than they ever have been in or since I've been

01:14:36.480 --> 01:14:39.560
flying. Although change is good,

01:14:39.560 --> 01:14:44.800
sometimes you have to accept that actually what we have or

01:14:44.800 --> 01:14:49.120
what we had was a really good thing, you know, and for sure

01:14:49.120 --> 01:14:52.520
there's always ways that we can improve.

01:14:52.520 --> 01:14:55.280
There's always ways that we can make it safer.

01:14:56.000 --> 01:14:58.360
But often does that make it better?

01:14:58.800 --> 01:15:00.080
I don't know. I don't know.

01:15:00.080 --> 01:15:04.240
I think that's where we all have our own individual limit and

01:15:04.240 --> 01:15:08.320
line. But any, any movement in the

01:15:08.320 --> 01:15:11.840
world in the, in the for safety is good.

01:15:11.840 --> 01:15:13.880
But it, it has its limits, doesn't it?

01:15:13.880 --> 01:15:19.240
I mean, it has its limits where and how far we go with the

01:15:19.240 --> 01:15:22.000
rules. For example, for example, the

01:15:22.000 --> 01:15:25.720
CCC rule was, was really good in its day and it did its job,

01:15:25.720 --> 01:15:28.400
which for me was let's limit top speed.

01:15:28.400 --> 01:15:32.560
We, we, we, we have to go slower because the wings were so on

01:15:32.560 --> 01:15:36.960
edge when they blew out at 70 odd kilometres an hour, it was

01:15:36.960 --> 01:15:41.000
always missing. So getting rid of trimmers and

01:15:41.000 --> 01:15:44.160
getting reducing top speed, I think did a really good thing

01:15:44.160 --> 01:15:50.160
for, for, for pilot safety. But as you know, as Luke has so

01:15:50.160 --> 01:15:53.640
eloquently pointed out in in in the interview with you and, and

01:15:53.640 --> 01:15:57.160
then in many other discussions, is that those limits and those

01:15:57.160 --> 01:16:03.040
rules have created a point where, OK, this is the profile

01:16:03.040 --> 01:16:06.240
that you need to get the maximum speed out of 14 centimetres of

01:16:06.240 --> 01:16:09.040
travel. And if we want to improve

01:16:09.440 --> 01:16:13.240
stability, then we have to reduce top speed.

01:16:14.320 --> 01:16:19.960
We can only improve stability by being and maintain the same top

01:16:19.960 --> 01:16:22.920
speed for that stability by increasing the speed range.

01:16:23.200 --> 01:16:27.440
So it's although we want to make more stable wings given the

01:16:27.520 --> 01:16:31.200
given the rules that we have at the moment, if we were to do so,

01:16:31.480 --> 01:16:35.280
we would have less top speed, which would make us not

01:16:35.280 --> 01:16:38.640
competitive. So the current rules are forcing

01:16:38.640 --> 01:16:42.880
designers into kind of 1 optimal kind of design.

01:16:43.960 --> 01:16:49.840
And I think that's why Luke and others are pushing to, to change

01:16:49.840 --> 01:16:54.400
the, the, the limiter, the 14 centimetres limiter because that

01:16:54.400 --> 01:16:58.880
allows to go to, to more stable profiles.

01:16:59.760 --> 01:17:04.200
Their stable profiles is, is something that we're moving

01:17:04.200 --> 01:17:07.200
towards and we have done for the last 15 years.

01:17:07.200 --> 01:17:12.640
And when we first in the old days of paragliding, you know,

01:17:12.640 --> 01:17:16.120
we, we have quite highly cambered profiles and we needed

01:17:16.120 --> 01:17:18.280
it to overcome all the drag that we had.

01:17:18.680 --> 01:17:23.240
We also had really flat arcs and a combination of high camber and

01:17:23.240 --> 01:17:28.120
flat arc made super aggressive behaviour post collapse.

01:17:29.120 --> 01:17:33.000
And they also weren't very resistant to collapse, but

01:17:33.000 --> 01:17:35.880
that's what was required to be able to not land at the bottom

01:17:35.880 --> 01:17:37.560
of the hill back in the early 90s.

01:17:39.280 --> 01:17:42.960
As we've evolved, as performance has got better, as drag has

01:17:42.960 --> 01:17:48.520
reduced, the arc has increased, which is helped for post

01:17:48.520 --> 01:17:50.680
collapse behaviour, cycle collapse behaviour.

01:17:52.640 --> 01:17:56.800
And it's allowed for more stable profiles as well.

01:17:59.000 --> 01:18:03.120
But everything you know, has has has its limits with within the

01:18:03.120 --> 01:18:06.480
current rules. I think I think we all agree,

01:18:06.480 --> 01:18:09.000
like you were saying earlier, you know, when you after you

01:18:09.000 --> 01:18:10.840
threw your reserve, you're like, oh, why don't a family hang

01:18:10.840 --> 01:18:14.320
glider that doesn't collapse? And we've all dreamt of, you

01:18:14.320 --> 01:18:16.360
know, the paraglider that doesn't collapse.

01:18:16.360 --> 01:18:21.480
And modern designers are kind of going more in this direction to

01:18:21.480 --> 01:18:26.600
try and make stronger profiles, more collapse resistance,

01:18:26.880 --> 01:18:30.280
because we believe that is safer and it is safer.

01:18:30.280 --> 01:18:33.080
I'd much prefer to fly a wing that didn't collapse the one

01:18:33.080 --> 01:18:38.240
that did. But we're still, we still have

01:18:38.240 --> 01:18:45.600
to work within the realms of certification which favours

01:18:45.760 --> 01:18:48.320
wings that are easy to collapse rather than wings that are

01:18:48.320 --> 01:18:52.160
difficult to collapse. So I think we, we're in a

01:18:52.160 --> 01:18:59.680
process now where I think in the next 1520 years we'll see some

01:18:59.680 --> 01:19:05.120
more reasonable, I'd like to say radical, but I don't think the

01:19:05.120 --> 01:19:09.360
WG Six group, which, which made the rules for certification are

01:19:09.360 --> 01:19:12.680
a radical bunch. So I think we'll, I think we'll

01:19:12.680 --> 01:19:17.040
see changes in the future to the certification process to make it

01:19:17.680 --> 01:19:20.240
easier for stronger profile wins to get through.

01:19:22.280 --> 01:19:27.600
Zillion points that I want to diverge to, but before I want to

01:19:27.600 --> 01:19:32.640
go, let's say Will Gad mentioned because I've had him on the show

01:19:32.640 --> 01:19:35.560
in the early days as well. Another gem of a production.

01:19:37.160 --> 01:19:43.000
He said the ones who make the change, who will make the change

01:19:43.000 --> 01:19:46.440
are the ones who go and challenge the the status quo and

01:19:46.440 --> 01:19:49.160
you know, are like the gung ho about it and stuff like that.

01:19:49.800 --> 01:19:53.920
So it's the minority that will eventually push us towards

01:19:54.880 --> 01:19:58.520
getting out of a comfort zone to make sure that we are heading

01:19:58.520 --> 01:20:01.720
forward and not stagnating ourselves or slipping backwards.

01:20:02.680 --> 01:20:08.120
When I saw two liner E&C's coming out, yes, they were

01:20:08.120 --> 01:20:12.440
serving very well to the racing community.

01:20:13.240 --> 01:20:18.240
But when you compare the racing community to the majority of

01:20:18.800 --> 01:20:22.680
consumers in paragliding, there are only a handful of pilots.

01:20:23.600 --> 01:20:28.680
I thought we were doing a huge disservice to every single

01:20:28.800 --> 01:20:33.840
intermediate pilot that had the option of going from a lobby to

01:20:33.840 --> 01:20:38.120
a three liner ENC because suddenly three liner Encs were

01:20:38.120 --> 01:20:45.120
not that lucrative anymore. So when we are allowing a small

01:20:45.120 --> 01:20:50.920
minority to make the change for when it comes to ENC class, why

01:20:50.920 --> 01:20:54.960
aren't we letting the same thought process push or

01:20:54.960 --> 01:20:58.840
spearhead at the open or triple C levels?

01:20:58.840 --> 01:21:02.360
Because there we want to pull back, but here we want to push

01:21:02.360 --> 01:21:05.320
forward. So it kind of baffles to me that

01:21:06.520 --> 01:21:13.200
are the ones at the helm making certain decisions which are in

01:21:14.160 --> 01:21:17.640
like hidden interests. I don't want to rub across

01:21:17.640 --> 01:21:22.320
anyone in a bad way but it just seems commercial market

01:21:23.360 --> 01:21:27.240
viability be at the forefront of such busy working rather than

01:21:27.800 --> 01:21:32.080
the safe or like the fun factor. Well, I just want to push back

01:21:32.640 --> 01:21:36.000
on how, whether you consider because I, I totally understand

01:21:36.000 --> 01:21:41.480
when when 2 liner encs came on the market and everyone thinks,

01:21:41.480 --> 01:21:43.760
oh, we're pushing in the wrong direction.

01:21:44.600 --> 01:21:48.800
But you just need to think about the previous generation or

01:21:48.800 --> 01:21:53.440
especially the generation of wins that was previous to to the

01:21:53.440 --> 01:21:57.400
to the generation that was superseded by the two liners and

01:21:57.440 --> 01:22:00.720
some of those wings. You could look at the leading

01:22:00.720 --> 01:22:02.360
edge and it would collapse at full speed.

01:22:05.160 --> 01:22:09.000
They were they were trimmed in such a way that they were

01:22:09.000 --> 01:22:13.480
designed for certification. They worked well in calm air,

01:22:13.480 --> 01:22:17.640
but as soon as you're pushing along a Ridge in the mountains,

01:22:17.640 --> 01:22:20.400
they they're not very interesting to fly because they

01:22:20.400 --> 01:22:24.200
were collapsing all the time. You had to constantly fly active

01:22:24.200 --> 01:22:26.520
coming off the bar on the bar with your legs.

01:22:26.520 --> 01:22:32.520
And it was a very evolving, very demanding wing to fly.

01:22:34.520 --> 01:22:37.200
And then everyone thinks it's two liner, it's two liner, it's

01:22:37.200 --> 01:22:42.320
difficult, it's dangerous. It's it's much harder, but and,

01:22:43.200 --> 01:22:45.680
but that's not the case. I think what you find now with

01:22:45.680 --> 01:22:48.440
two liners is that they're easier to fly for an experienced

01:22:48.440 --> 01:22:50.840
pilot. They're easier, less demanding

01:22:50.840 --> 01:22:55.040
to fly than the old generation 3 liner Encs.

01:22:55.320 --> 01:23:00.600
They're inherently more stable because the profiles got a bit

01:23:00.680 --> 01:23:04.000
less CM in it, a bit more CM in it.

01:23:04.000 --> 01:23:07.320
Sorry. And not only that, but they're

01:23:07.320 --> 01:23:10.160
easier to control because you've got the wing in your hands at

01:23:10.160 --> 01:23:12.960
all time and you've got direct angle of attack control with the

01:23:12.960 --> 01:23:18.000
B risers. So to me it was a no brainer.

01:23:18.000 --> 01:23:20.240
To me, it's it's an improvement in every way.

01:23:20.240 --> 01:23:25.320
And I think now if you speak to a lot of the guys that were

01:23:25.320 --> 01:23:28.480
maybe a bit more reluctant to begin with, they're very, very

01:23:28.480 --> 01:23:30.240
happy. I think that the success of the

01:23:30.240 --> 01:23:35.120
SRS series and that that level of wing is, is testament to

01:23:35.120 --> 01:23:38.080
that. You know, we, we, we were also

01:23:38.640 --> 01:23:42.360
cautious as well. We released the LP, we, we re

01:23:42.800 --> 01:23:47.440
we, we remodelled the Alpena 4 so that we, we also offered a

01:23:47.440 --> 01:23:50.840
three liner E&C wing out there as well.

01:23:52.720 --> 01:23:55.160
But I don't think there was a very successful product.

01:23:55.160 --> 01:24:02.960
Most pilots who fly E&C have moved over to the, the more the

01:24:02.960 --> 01:24:06.760
two liners these days because it's a win, win situation.

01:24:06.760 --> 01:24:10.400
I think for me, the, the, for me, the perfect balance is, is

01:24:10.400 --> 01:24:12.560
low aspect ratio and to line of control.

01:24:12.560 --> 01:24:15.760
To me, that's just like what we should all be flying and where

01:24:15.920 --> 01:24:18.560
we should all go eventually, where all experienced pilots

01:24:18.560 --> 01:24:23.480
should probably end up flying. Because the fact that we can use

01:24:23.480 --> 01:24:26.960
collapse lines makes it mean that we can use profiles that

01:24:26.960 --> 01:24:30.240
are slightly stronger. So they're, they're inherently

01:24:30.240 --> 01:24:33.960
more stable profiles. They're less likely to collapse.

01:24:34.480 --> 01:24:37.160
That can also you can trim them in a way that actually makes

01:24:37.160 --> 01:24:39.200
them a little bit more comfortable as well.

01:24:39.960 --> 01:24:42.960
And because you've got the the bees, you've always got control

01:24:42.960 --> 01:24:45.440
of the wing no matter what speed you're going at.

01:24:45.440 --> 01:24:52.080
So I think that was a real step forward in both performance and

01:24:52.080 --> 01:24:54.800
in safety for experienced pilots.

01:24:55.880 --> 01:24:57.200
Feel free to correct me if I'm wrong.

01:24:57.200 --> 01:25:02.600
Russ experienced pilot versus the majority which is a weakened

01:25:02.600 --> 01:25:05.880
flyer. Is my dilemma correct or is it

01:25:05.880 --> 01:25:10.720
just in my head that we are catering to the ones who are

01:25:10.720 --> 01:25:14.120
pushing the limits? And if that is the case, then

01:25:14.120 --> 01:25:17.400
why did we ban open class? No, I don't think we are

01:25:17.400 --> 01:25:19.040
catering to those pushing the limits.

01:25:19.040 --> 01:25:23.360
I think we've always been and the whole, you know, the, the,

01:25:23.640 --> 01:25:27.440
the political organisations behind paragliding the WG 6, the

01:25:27.440 --> 01:25:31.600
PMI, the, the national governing bodies, they've, they've always

01:25:32.480 --> 01:25:35.240
moved and done everything that's in the best for the majority of

01:25:35.240 --> 01:25:38.480
the majority of their pilots. And the majority of the pilots

01:25:38.520 --> 01:25:44.040
are weekend pilots. If I look, if I think about the,

01:25:44.040 --> 01:25:47.360
the UK flying community, which is, which is on closest to, you

01:25:47.360 --> 01:25:49.560
know, there's a lot of experienced pilots there.

01:25:49.720 --> 01:25:53.200
They may be only flying at the weekends, but they they form the

01:25:53.200 --> 01:25:58.880
majority of the the pilot base, the beginners and the

01:25:58.880 --> 01:26:02.400
intermediates are a slightly smaller proportion.

01:26:02.440 --> 01:26:06.360
They probably make up probably 45% of that group.

01:26:08.400 --> 01:26:13.080
And we and WG 6, which is, which is the organization that makes

01:26:13.080 --> 01:26:17.560
the rules for the EN testing. They're very much aware of this

01:26:17.560 --> 01:26:24.480
and that's why we keep the Ena and the ENB in very simple three

01:26:24.480 --> 01:26:27.920
liner type wings that we can't use collapse lines with.

01:26:27.960 --> 01:26:36.000
But the the irony of that is that the two liners are now more

01:26:36.000 --> 01:26:40.240
stable than the beginner wings. A beginner wing is more likely

01:26:40.240 --> 01:26:44.400
to collapse in turbulent air than a high performance wing if

01:26:44.400 --> 01:26:48.960
with no input. So we're in a situation where

01:26:49.640 --> 01:26:53.840
beginner low airtime pilots have to fly the inherently less

01:26:53.840 --> 01:26:56.160
stable wings than the more experienced pilots.

01:26:57.120 --> 01:27:00.560
But that's just the nature of the certification procedures at

01:27:00.600 --> 01:27:02.600
the moment and the rules that we currently have.

01:27:03.160 --> 01:27:05.320
I had Michael Nessler on the show as well.

01:27:05.480 --> 01:27:10.720
He had come out with, I wouldn't say like truly 2 liner, but he

01:27:10.720 --> 01:27:13.840
had found a way to put collapse. Yeah.

01:27:14.480 --> 01:27:18.400
Yeah, so 2 liner B, of course, it's very enticing, you know, to

01:27:18.400 --> 01:27:22.880
any pilot who wants to push the limits and kind of go about it.

01:27:22.880 --> 01:27:26.600
And as a designer, he did his own thing to quench his

01:27:26.600 --> 01:27:32.320
curiosity. But is 2 liner is like like what

01:27:32.320 --> 01:27:35.400
is a trade off if A2 liner A comes on the market or is, is

01:27:35.400 --> 01:27:39.120
that a fair bit of evolution that the industry will go

01:27:39.120 --> 01:27:40.960
towards? Because yeah, you, you keep on

01:27:40.960 --> 01:27:44.080
pushing the flight envelope as the year's progress, more or

01:27:44.080 --> 01:27:47.800
less. At the moment, I think the one

01:27:47.800 --> 01:27:51.160
thing we can't cheat, I mean, two line, three line, 4 line, it

01:27:51.160 --> 01:27:52.800
doesn't matter. We were having these same

01:27:52.800 --> 01:27:55.600
discussions when we went from four line to three line years

01:27:55.600 --> 01:27:59.960
ago, you know, exactly the same discussion that we're having

01:27:59.960 --> 01:28:01.800
when we went from three line to two line.

01:28:03.680 --> 01:28:07.960
I think what's what, what we don't, what we can't limit is

01:28:07.960 --> 01:28:13.680
aspect ratio. Aspect ratio is the main factor

01:28:13.800 --> 01:28:18.800
that defines the behaviour and the demand on the pilot in

01:28:18.800 --> 01:28:22.640
terminal here, if you look at every manufacturer, their

01:28:22.640 --> 01:28:25.520
beginner wings have a lower aspect ratio than their high

01:28:25.520 --> 01:28:29.000
performance wings. There's very, I can't think of

01:28:29.000 --> 01:28:33.400
any manufacturer that kind of, you know, that the ENB has a

01:28:33.560 --> 01:28:35.840
higher aspect ratio than their ENC.

01:28:35.840 --> 01:28:39.560
It just doesn't really happen unless there's some specialist

01:28:39.560 --> 01:28:44.400
mounting wings or something. Aspect ratio is the most

01:28:44.400 --> 01:28:48.680
important factor in very turbulent air.

01:28:49.320 --> 01:28:52.440
When the wing, when the when you're in really turbulent air

01:28:52.440 --> 01:28:56.400
and the wing is making shapes and moving in pitch and loses

01:28:56.400 --> 01:29:00.840
its span cohesion, aspect ratio takes over.

01:29:00.840 --> 01:29:04.320
And that's why comp wings are a bit more demand, more demand to

01:29:04.320 --> 01:29:07.760
fly than E&D wings and E&D wings are more than E&C.

01:29:08.280 --> 01:29:11.680
When we get down to E&B, we're getting into a realms where we

01:29:12.080 --> 01:29:14.120
current knowledge and technology, well, not

01:29:14.120 --> 01:29:16.120
technology, but current knowledge.

01:29:16.120 --> 01:29:20.000
I'd say we're struggling. We, we, we can't make super low

01:29:20.040 --> 01:29:24.360
aspect ratio with two lines and still support the whole

01:29:24.360 --> 01:29:26.840
structure very well. You know something's going to

01:29:26.840 --> 01:29:30.040
give you, you either have to have a massive gap between the,

01:29:30.560 --> 01:29:34.720
the A's and the B's, in which case it will be hard to control

01:29:34.720 --> 01:29:38.320
the, the the cord unless we start bugging in a load of

01:29:38.360 --> 01:29:41.760
carbon or something. And if you have a big gap

01:29:41.760 --> 01:29:44.800
between the, the bees and the trailing edge, then you just got

01:29:44.800 --> 01:29:47.680
this big floppy unsupported thing at the back of the wing,

01:29:47.680 --> 01:29:53.120
which will give you horrible structure and vague breaks that

01:29:53.120 --> 01:29:58.120
won't be very nice to fly. So aspect ratio limits where we

01:29:58.120 --> 01:30:02.000
can go with two liners at the moment, unless you start bugging

01:30:02.000 --> 01:30:04.080
in a load of rigid structure in the wing.

01:30:06.360 --> 01:30:09.520
But another factor as well for me and the big the big point for

01:30:09.520 --> 01:30:13.640
me, because we did have collapse lines in E and BA few years ago,

01:30:14.440 --> 01:30:21.000
but that was banned. What's nice about E&B is a is a

01:30:21.680 --> 01:30:26.600
is a place for up and coming pilots to, to to go to is a.

01:30:27.360 --> 01:30:30.720
You can have the most exquisite handling.

01:30:31.200 --> 01:30:35.480
The best tightest turning turning radius is probably on a

01:30:35.480 --> 01:30:41.080
high performance E&B wing. A good pilot on a on a the A&B

01:30:41.080 --> 01:30:44.120
wing will be able to turn inside anyone or a comp wing.

01:30:44.960 --> 01:30:46.880
So your climb rate can be amazing.

01:30:46.920 --> 01:30:50.560
The handling is really fun. They're like the Porsche 9/11 of

01:30:50.560 --> 01:30:57.080
our of our flying machines. So it's actually a really cool

01:30:57.080 --> 01:31:00.800
place for relatively experienced.

01:31:00.800 --> 01:31:03.360
When I say that, I mean you'd need to have like 100 hours to

01:31:03.360 --> 01:31:07.560
be flying a top end high end B at least and maybe done an SIV

01:31:07.560 --> 01:31:11.520
course. But you find a real sweet spot

01:31:11.520 --> 01:31:17.080
in in in pure flying pleasure with the E&B wings.

01:31:17.480 --> 01:31:21.640
And with that wing you can then progress on to doing cross

01:31:21.640 --> 01:31:27.280
country acro SIV. You can really learn your trade

01:31:28.040 --> 01:31:33.320
on these high end E&B wings and that gives you in good stead if

01:31:33.320 --> 01:31:36.800
you choose because many pilots choose not to go beyond that

01:31:36.800 --> 01:31:39.080
point. But if you really want to get

01:31:39.080 --> 01:31:43.160
into more performance and do more cross country, that's when

01:31:43.160 --> 01:31:45.960
it gives you a good stepping stone into the next level of

01:31:45.960 --> 01:31:48.960
wing up. So I think right now we're in

01:31:48.960 --> 01:31:50.600
the best position that we've ever been.

01:31:50.600 --> 01:31:54.280
When I think about back in the years of what the products that

01:31:54.280 --> 01:31:56.480
we had throughout all the different ranges for all the

01:31:56.480 --> 01:32:00.200
different pilots, I think right now looking at the range that

01:32:00.200 --> 01:32:02.840
Ozone have and looking at the range that other manufacturers

01:32:02.840 --> 01:32:06.920
have, it's a really good time to be a pilot because you've got

01:32:06.920 --> 01:32:10.120
such a good choice of different wings from different

01:32:10.120 --> 01:32:14.760
manufacturers and there's no, there's no real dogs out there

01:32:14.760 --> 01:32:17.080
anymore. So it's all good.

01:32:17.080 --> 01:32:19.760
It's all healthy, I think. Totally agree.

01:32:19.760 --> 01:32:22.800
And I think with the with this discussion, we answered the

01:32:22.800 --> 01:32:27.960
question more or less about how to upgrade from a 2 1/2 or three

01:32:27.960 --> 01:32:31.640
liner E&B to A2 liner C for any pilot who is wandering out

01:32:31.640 --> 01:32:36.440
there, You know, because like, yeah, as long as stepping on the

01:32:36.440 --> 01:32:40.160
stepping stone feels comfortable and you've done your homework,

01:32:40.920 --> 01:32:45.920
just like, yeah, feel free to explore the more exciting world,

01:32:45.920 --> 01:32:49.760
I would say, which is not just more stable, but also more fun

01:32:49.800 --> 01:32:53.440
and expands your flight envelope by a whole lot further.

01:32:54.400 --> 01:32:57.560
Yeah, I think, you know, we got a lot of feedback at Ozone.

01:32:57.560 --> 01:33:00.960
I get quite a lot of the feedback at Ozone and a lot of

01:33:00.960 --> 01:33:04.200
people who were really concerned about the photon, for example,

01:33:04.200 --> 01:33:07.560
when we released that were pleasantly surprised.

01:33:07.840 --> 01:33:12.040
Everyone was I had no feedback saying, OK, yeah, this is too

01:33:12.040 --> 01:33:14.640
much for me. I I want to go back to its four

01:33:14.640 --> 01:33:18.480
liner Enci don't recall any anything like that.

01:33:18.480 --> 01:33:21.200
The vast majority of the feedback that we had was, was

01:33:21.200 --> 01:33:25.520
really positive in, in terms of how comfortable people are, how

01:33:25.560 --> 01:33:29.200
intuitive it was to fly 2 liners, how easy it was.

01:33:29.200 --> 01:33:33.160
And they wish they'd made that step earlier.

01:33:33.920 --> 01:33:36.880
And the same thing when we released, we when we recently

01:33:36.880 --> 01:33:39.560
released the Delta last year, which was the lower aspect, the

01:33:39.560 --> 01:33:44.720
NC, we again, there was a lot of die hard Delta pilots that were

01:33:45.400 --> 01:33:47.160
a little bit wrecked. They were like, what do you mean

01:33:47.160 --> 01:33:50.000
you've changed it to A2 liner? You've ruined my Delta

01:33:50.000 --> 01:33:54.360
experience. And pretty much every one of

01:33:54.360 --> 01:33:56.840
them have come back to me and said wow, this is amazing, this

01:33:56.840 --> 01:33:58.800
is brilliant. This is way better than all the

01:33:58.800 --> 01:34:03.040
other deltas that I've had. And so I think once you kind of,

01:34:03.800 --> 01:34:09.760
you know this, there's a lot of and I understand why and because

01:34:09.760 --> 01:34:13.240
the early days, the first generation of two liners were,

01:34:14.160 --> 01:34:17.280
were difficult, They were demanding and we haven't quite

01:34:17.280 --> 01:34:21.000
worked it all out. And they were unpleasant.

01:34:21.160 --> 01:34:25.000
They had some unpleasant behaviour and were difficult to

01:34:25.000 --> 01:34:30.480
fly and that gave 2 liners a bad reputation, a bad Rep over the

01:34:30.480 --> 01:34:32.520
years. Whereas now with the

01:34:32.520 --> 01:34:34.840
understanding that we've had, we've had two liners have been

01:34:34.840 --> 01:34:41.240
around 16 years now and in that time the designers have, have

01:34:41.400 --> 01:34:44.680
have learnt to make them much better.

01:34:44.680 --> 01:34:47.240
And, and, and part of the testing process as well.

01:34:47.240 --> 01:34:50.520
We understand the behaviour a little bit better to a point now

01:34:50.520 --> 01:34:57.120
where if you, if you blindfold me and I had to do a full stall

01:34:57.120 --> 01:35:00.480
or a spin, I wouldn't know if it's a 2 liner or a three liner.

01:35:00.520 --> 01:35:03.640
If you, if, if you, if someone else collapsed it.

01:35:03.640 --> 01:35:05.080
For me, I'm not. Obviously I can feel what I'm

01:35:05.080 --> 01:35:08.480
collapsing in my hands, but if someone collapsed I can't tell

01:35:08.480 --> 01:35:12.920
the difference between a collapse on A2 liner or three

01:35:12.920 --> 01:35:15.360
liner given if everything else was the same.

01:35:15.720 --> 01:35:17.680
There is no difference in behaviour.

01:35:17.880 --> 01:35:21.720
I've come to the conclusion with with the modern generation of of

01:35:21.720 --> 01:35:25.200
two liners. Wow, I I've always had this

01:35:25.200 --> 01:35:29.040
notion that two liners just went like less often, but they when

01:35:29.040 --> 01:35:31.400
they did they was they just went a lot more harder.

01:35:31.400 --> 01:35:33.800
But that's such a relief to hear now.

01:35:34.160 --> 01:35:35.880
No, I don't think that's the case.

01:35:35.880 --> 01:35:39.760
No, we the wings that went the hardest with the old fashioned

01:35:39.760 --> 01:35:43.760
comp wings, they, they, they used to go horrible paragliders

01:35:44.080 --> 01:35:46.200
throughout over the years have gone bad.

01:35:46.240 --> 01:35:49.720
You know, what tends to happen though, when you do have a wing

01:35:49.720 --> 01:35:53.200
that's more inherently stable is that it is the really hard

01:35:54.520 --> 01:35:59.040
turbulence that collapses them. And so when you do get the

01:35:59.040 --> 01:36:01.680
collapse, it's normally because of very aggressive hard

01:36:01.680 --> 01:36:05.560
turbulence and you go, oh, that goes harder than the old ones

01:36:05.560 --> 01:36:06.840
used to. But the old ones used to be

01:36:06.840 --> 01:36:09.600
going in all turbulence and would go equally and hard in

01:36:09.600 --> 01:36:14.400
that same given turbulence. So you do get less collapses,

01:36:14.400 --> 01:36:21.840
which is why you can become, you know, a little bit less practice

01:36:21.840 --> 01:36:25.120
that those collapses and the collapses do come a little bit

01:36:25.120 --> 01:36:27.720
stronger because it's the stronger air that's making them

01:36:27.960 --> 01:36:37.640
elapse that way. But old high, high CM wings

01:36:37.640 --> 01:36:40.080
would would collapse equally bad in those conditions.

01:36:40.120 --> 01:36:44.160
Reserve deployments whether or not the the coefficient, moment

01:36:44.160 --> 01:36:46.760
coefficient, the reflex, the amount of reflex in the way.

01:36:47.800 --> 01:36:50.200
Right, there's a lot of technical jargon which like

01:36:50.200 --> 01:36:53.160
layman does not understand. What's so interesting?

01:36:53.320 --> 01:36:55.840
Hearing a professional talk about it like that.

01:36:55.920 --> 01:36:57.640
Yeah. Well, like I was saying, like I

01:36:57.640 --> 01:37:02.680
was saying earlier, we went from, you know, very lifty

01:37:02.680 --> 01:37:06.960
profiles that we had negative CM.

01:37:06.960 --> 01:37:12.000
So they, their tendency is nose down and we're now going towards

01:37:12.000 --> 01:37:16.720
more reflex type profiles, which has a nose up tendency in, in,

01:37:17.000 --> 01:37:21.280
in its movement, which which makes the wings more inherently

01:37:21.280 --> 01:37:26.520
stable, but it does make them more difficult to get through

01:37:26.640 --> 01:37:29.800
certification. But, but going back to the two

01:37:29.800 --> 01:37:35.120
liner, I, I, I really strongly believe that with the latest

01:37:35.120 --> 01:37:38.200
generation and I can only really talk about the wins that we've

01:37:38.200 --> 01:37:40.480
made and the wins I've personally developed.

01:37:41.560 --> 01:37:46.120
But I think we got to a point there where the difference in

01:37:46.120 --> 01:37:50.440
behaviour between two line or three line has has merged into

01:37:50.440 --> 01:37:54.600
insignificance. And in terms of safety, I see

01:37:54.600 --> 01:37:58.240
the two liner for the right pilot for an experienced pilot

01:37:58.240 --> 01:38:01.160
as an easier more comfortable winter for.

01:38:02.400 --> 01:38:05.360
I remember you were like you know earlier a few, a few like

01:38:05.600 --> 01:38:09.000
moments ago you said that it's quite an amazing time to be a

01:38:09.000 --> 01:38:11.440
paraglider pilot with the amount of offerings that are there in

01:38:11.440 --> 01:38:14.320
the industry. The question comes to my mind,

01:38:14.320 --> 01:38:21.120
is your generation one of the only ones who has seen the most

01:38:21.120 --> 01:38:23.440
amount of development in our industry?

01:38:23.440 --> 01:38:29.760
Because you guys have seen it from absolute chaos to, I

01:38:29.760 --> 01:38:32.760
wouldn't say butter smooth, but like we're in, I don't know,

01:38:32.880 --> 01:38:37.240
like that's a lot of evolution that has gone down in two or

01:38:37.240 --> 01:38:41.000
three decades where you started flying and now you're like, you

01:38:41.000 --> 01:38:43.240
know. Yeah, I mean that, I mean,

01:38:43.240 --> 01:38:46.520
paragliding only really existed in the mid 80s, didn't it, with

01:38:46.560 --> 01:38:49.080
people jumping off with parachutes.

01:38:49.080 --> 01:38:52.480
And then it's developed into what we have today.

01:38:52.480 --> 01:38:58.520
And you know, that's, I think it's, it's down to, like you

01:38:58.520 --> 01:39:01.920
said, the minority pushing it. You know, when the when the SAT

01:39:01.920 --> 01:39:06.560
theme came on the on the on the scene and they were pushing acro

01:39:06.560 --> 01:39:10.080
and that was a period when everyone said do acro, you know,

01:39:11.120 --> 01:39:14.720
the old pilots and the old pilots and all of that nonsense.

01:39:14.720 --> 01:39:19.560
And and then those boys came and I would just robbed it and all

01:39:19.560 --> 01:39:23.360
the old school were like, they'll die quickly.

01:39:23.760 --> 01:39:27.320
And they didn't. And they pushed the foot, the

01:39:27.320 --> 01:39:29.600
sport forward. They, they showed what was

01:39:29.600 --> 01:39:34.280
possible with those old comp wings and they created the whole

01:39:34.280 --> 01:39:37.240
acro scene, which is narrowing into an acro world tour and

01:39:37.240 --> 01:39:40.440
people doing tricks that I can't even imagine.

01:39:42.600 --> 01:39:47.160
And look at the comp scene now. You know, from, from I remember

01:39:47.160 --> 01:39:50.120
the first comp scene I used to do be like, all I'd worry about

01:39:50.120 --> 01:39:53.480
was don't land at the bottom of the hill, you know, don't land

01:39:53.480 --> 01:39:56.480
at the bottom. And a good day is when you

01:39:56.480 --> 01:39:59.240
didn't land at the bottom, whereas now I don't even think

01:39:59.240 --> 01:40:01.880
about landing at the bottom. And we're, we're, we're doing

01:40:02.520 --> 01:40:06.480
the amazing flying. In fact, flooring has become in

01:40:06.480 --> 01:40:08.760
the mountains. Racing paragliders for me in the

01:40:08.760 --> 01:40:13.160
mountains has become. Boring is not the right word,

01:40:13.160 --> 01:40:16.720
but just way too predictable. It's way too linear.

01:40:16.760 --> 01:40:19.040
It's like, OK, we're flying in the mountains.

01:40:19.040 --> 01:40:21.360
